<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maurice Ravel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Joseph Maurice Ravel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maurice Ravel was a French composer, pianist and conductor, born in the Basque town of Ciboure near Biarritz, who is often associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/impressionism">Impressionism</a> alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-debussy">Claude Debussy</a>, though both rejected the term. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Conservatoire he was not well regarded by its conservative establishment, whose biased treatment of him caused a scandal, and he went on to develop a style of great clarity drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">modernism</a>, baroque, neoclassicism and later jazz. His works include Gaspard de la nuit (1908), Daphnis et Chloé (1912), two piano concertos and his best-known piece, Boléro (1928), and his 1922 orchestration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modest-mussorgsky">Mussorgsky</a>'s Pictures at an Exhibition. He died in 1937.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maurice-ravel.jpg" alt="Maurice Ravel" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author The image holder, the Bibliothèque nationale de France has not identified a photographer. (Follow link and cli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurice_Ravel_1925.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph Maurice Ravel was born on 7 March 1875 in Ciboure, 18 kilometres from the Spanish border. His father, Pierre-Joseph Ravel, was an educated and successful engineer, inventor and manufacturer; among his inventions were an early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">internal combustion engine</a> and a circus machine called the Whirlwind of Death. His mother, Marie, née Delouart, was Basque but had grown up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. In November 1889 Ravel passed the examination for the preparatory piano class at the Paris Conservatoire, and he won first prize in its piano competition in 1891, though he did not otherwise stand out as a student.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Around 1900 Ravel joined an informal group of young artists and musicians known as Les Apaches, which at times included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-stravinsky">Igor Stravinsky</a> and Manuel de Falla. He made five attempts at the Prix de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 1905 he was eliminated in the first round, and the press was indignant when it emerged that only students of Charles Lenepveu, who sat on the jury, reached the final. L'affaire Ravel became a national scandal, leading to the early retirement of Dubois and his replacement by Fauré.</p><p>In March 1915 Ravel, rejected by the French Air Force for his age and a minor heart complaint, joined the Thirteenth Artillery Regiment as a lorry driver, at times driving munitions at night under bombardment. After the war his output, never large, became smaller, and he declined the Legion of Honour in 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Marcel Marnat's catalogue lists eighty-five works, about sixty of them performable, and Ravel wrote no symphonies or church works. Many exist first as a piano score and later as an orchestration, among them Ma mère l'Oye and Le tombeau de Couperin. In the early 1930s he completed the Piano Concerto in D major for the Left Hand for Paul Wittgenstein, who had lost his right arm in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>; Wittgenstein premiered it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in January 1932.</p><p>Ravel declined all French state honours but accepted an honorary doctorate from the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> in 1928. After his death his brother Édouard turned the house at Montfort-l'Amaury into a museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maurice Ravel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Musical Impressionism, characterised by suggestion and atmosphere, arose in France in the late 19th century; Claude Debussy and <b>Maurice Ravel</b> are generally considered its greatest composers, though Debussy called the term the invention of critics.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Composers from Carl Maria von Weber to Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov, whose Scheherazade dates from 1888, and <b>Maurice Ravel</b> set its stories, and illustrators from Gustave Doré to Edmund Dulac drew them.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
